## 11. TESTING & VALIDATION STRATEGY

### 11.1 Test Matrix

| Test Type | What It Validates | When to Run | Engine |
|-----------|------------------|-------------|--------|
| **Unit Tests** | Abstraction layer classes, DriverFactory, ArtifactManager | Every commit | N/A |
| **Integration Tests** | Browser launch, navigation, screenshots, video | Every PR | Both engines |
| **Script Generation Tests** | LLM generates valid Python, syntax correctness | Phase 4+ | Both engines |
| **Execution Tests** | Full test lifecycle: generate → execute → result | Phase 5+ | Both engines |
| **Self-Healing Tests** | Intentionally broken selectors heal correctly | Phase 6+ | Playwright |
| **Concurrency Tests** | 5-10 concurrent browser sessions | Phase 7+ | Playwright |
| **WebSocket Tests** | Real-time events stream correctly | All phases | N/A |
| **Regression Tests** | Existing test results match after migration | All phases | Both engines |
| **Performance Tests** | Execution speed, memory usage, throughput | Phase 7+ | Both engines |
| **Docker Tests** | Build, start, execute in container | Phase 9+ | Playwright |

### 11.2 Migration Validation Checklist

#### Pre-Migration
- [ ] All existing Selenium tests have baseline results recorded
- [ ] Execution success rate baseline documented
- [ ] Average execution duration baseline documented
- [ ] Memory usage baseline under load documented

#### Phase 3 Gate (Dual Engine)
- [ ] DriverFactory creates both Selenium and Playwright drivers
- [ ] Both drivers can navigate to a URL and take a screenshot
- [ ] API accepts `engine` parameter without breaking existing calls
- [ ] `BROWSER_ENGINE=selenium` produces identical behavior to pre-migration

#### Phase 5 Gate (Execution Engine)
- [ ] Playwright-generated scripts execute successfully via subprocess
- [ ] Screenshots captured on failure
- [ ] Video recorded via Playwright context
- [ ] Metadata (BROWSER, BROWSER_VERSION, OS_INFO) parsed from stdout
- [ ] WebSocket events fire correctly during execution
- [ ] Batch execution works with Playwright engine
- [ ] Scheduled execution works with Playwright engine

#### Phase 6 Gate (Self-Healing)
- [ ] `fix_failures()` generates valid Playwright repair scripts
- [ ] Self-healing success rate ≥ Selenium baseline
- [ ] DOM snapshot included in repair prompt
- [ ] Trace files generated for failed tests

#### Phase 10 Gate (Full Migration)
- [ ] All tests pass with `BROWSER_ENGINE=playwright`
- [ ] No references to `selenium` in active code paths
- [ ] Docker image builds and runs successfully
- [ ] Memory usage ≤ Selenium baseline
- [ ] Execution speed ≥ Selenium baseline (should be faster)
- [ ] All 11 API routes function correctly

### 11.3 Rollout Verification Checklist

- [ ] Health check endpoint responds with engine info
- [ ] WebSocket connections establish successfully
- [ ] Autonomous mode completes full cycle (analyze → report)
- [ ] Single test execution produces screenshot on failure
- [ ] Batch execution processes all tests in suite
- [ ] Scheduled test fires on time
- [ ] Self-healing triggers on failure and retries
- [ ] Test results saved to Supabase correctly
- [ ] Frontend displays execution status in real-time
- [ ] Artifacts (screenshots, video, traces) accessible via API

---

## 12. PRODUCTION READINESS AUDIT

### 12.1 Scoring Matrix (Post-Migration)

| Dimension | Current (Selenium) | Target (Playwright) | Score |
|-----------|-------------------|---------------------|-------|
| **Scalability** | 4/10 — single-threaded, no pooling | 8/10 — async, browser pool, concurrent | 🟢 |
| **Maintainability** | 5/10 — Selenium-specific code scattered | 8/10 — clean abstraction layer | 🟢 |
| **Reliability** | 5/10 — flaky waits, ChromeDriver drift | 8/10 — auto-wait, bundled browsers | 🟢 |
| **Cloud Readiness** | 4/10 — manual Chrome setup in Docker | 9/10 — official images, K8s ready | 🟢 |
| **AI Robustness** | 5/10 — verbose prompts, unreliable scripts | 8/10 — simpler prompts, better selectors | 🟢 |
| **Deployment Complexity** | 3/10 — ChromeDriver management nightmare | 8/10 — `playwright install` and done | 🟢 |
| **Observability** | 4/10 — stdout parsing, manual screenshots | 8/10 — traces, HAR, built-in video | 🟢 |
| **Security** | 6/10 — subprocess sandboxing | 6/10 — same model (subprocess) | 🟡 |
| **Cost Efficiency** | 5/10 — one browser per test | 8/10 — browser pooling, context reuse | 🟢 |
| **Developer Experience** | 5/10 — complex setup, verbose API | 9/10 — clean async API, great docs | 🟢 |

**Overall**: 4.6/10 → **8.0/10** — a significant production-readiness improvement.

### 12.2 Remaining Risks Post-Migration

| Risk | Severity | Mitigation |
|------|----------|------------|
| **Untrusted AI-generated code execution** | High | Subprocess sandboxing (unchanged from current) |
| **LLM rate limiting under load** | Medium | Groq rate limiter already implemented; add queueing |
| **Single-node architecture** | Medium | Browser pool handles 10-20 concurrent tests; add Redis queue for more |
| **No execution queue/prioritization** | Medium | Implement arq/Celery when needed |
| **Playwright version upgrades** | Low | Pin version in requirements.txt + Docker image |
| **Supabase sync client** | Low | Migrate to async client separately |

### 12.3 Technical Debt Remaining

1. **Sync Supabase client** — should migrate to async SDK (separate effort)
2. **Sync LLM client** — should use `httpx` async HTTP client
3. **No execution queue** — need Redis + arq for production-scale
4. **No artifact storage** — should move to S3/MinIO for cloud deployment
5. **Hardcoded test timeout (60s)** — should be configurable per test
6. **No browser health monitoring** — add Prometheus metrics
7. **Single LLM provider** — add OpenAI/Anthropic fallback

### 12.4 Enterprise-Readiness Gaps

| Gap | Priority | Effort |
|-----|----------|--------|
| Multi-region deployment | Low | High — requires artifact replication |
| SSO/SAML authentication | Medium | Medium — Supabase supports this |
| Audit logging | Medium | Low — add middleware |
| Role-based access (admin/viewer/editor) | Medium | Medium — extend RLS policies |
| Test result retention policies | Low | Low — scheduled cleanup |
| API rate limiting | Medium | Low — FastAPI middleware |
| Execution cost tracking | Low | Medium — add metering |
| Custom browser profiles | Low | Low — Playwright supports this natively |

### 12.5 Architecture Decision Records

| Decision | Rationale | Alternative Considered |
|----------|-----------|----------------------|
| Keep subprocess execution model | AI-generated scripts are untrusted; process isolation is required | In-process `exec()` — rejected for security |
| Browser pool with semaphore | Simple, sufficient for 10-20 concurrent tests | Selenium Grid — rejected as over-complex |
| Maintain Selenium as fallback | Zero-risk rollback during migration | Hard cutover — rejected as too risky |
| Engine via env var, not per-request | Simpler operations; avoid engine mismatch in cached scripts | Per-request engine — deferred to post-migration |
| Rename DB table to `automation_scripts` | Future-proofs naming; any engine can store scripts | Keep `selenium_scripts` — rejected as confusing |
